This brings up an interesting point: What does a player look like throughout 
their lifetime? Do they get steadily better at the game? Or do they suddenly 
become much much better at some point?

If we examine a player's skill by looking at the maxDungeonLevel of their 
last 100 deaths, we can get a feel for the shape of a player. Here I've 
placed my first hundred games up against the tally of all games played
(taken from http://alt.org/nethack/statnethack/statnethack-d.txt )


Lvl| Me  | Avg
==============
01 | 32% | 21% \
02 | 17% | 12% |
03 | 22% | 14% | 3/4 of all deaths
04 | 13% | 12% |
05 | 09% | 09% |
06 | 02% | 07% /
07 | 02% | 04%
08 | 02% | 02%
09 | 00% | 02%
10 | 01% | 01%
11+| 00% | 16% (less than 1% on each level)

If we plot each span of 100 consecutive plays by a player, we can get some 
feel for a person's development/growth.

My first 100 games has the same general shape as the average. Many deaths on 
level one, more deaths on level three than level two, and then it rapidly 
drops off heading towards level ten. But my beginner "angle of decent" is 
steeper than the average. It is my guess that my second 100 games will have 
fewer deaths in the upper three levels, and more in levels six through ten.

However, if my improvement suddenly shifts from this pattern to maybe a much 
flatter line where my characters are dying at any random level below ten, 
then I would suspect a game imbalance, such as a superWeapon or 
UberProtection required to progress. 




